\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{A shifted product rule}
\lean{fwdDiff_smul}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{Let #{R} be a commutative unital ring, let #{n\ge1}, and take
#{V=\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z}, #{E=\{e_i:i\in V\}}, with
#{s(e_i)=i} and #{h(e_i)=i+1}. The specified edge-label bijection gives
an #{R}-algebra isomorphism
##{j:\mathcal F_R(E)\to\mathcal F_R(V),\qquad j(a)(i)=a(e_i).}
Its inverse sends #{f} to the edge field #{e_i\mapsto f(i)};
both preserve the pointwise operations. With the
\vocabk{ring-valued incidence map}{fdip-0010}, the
\newvocab{cyclic shift} and \newvocab{forward difference} are
##{(Tf)(i)=f(i+1),\qquad \Delta=jd=T-I.}
The shift is an algebra automorphism, with
#{(T^{-1}f)(i)=f(i-1)}. Vertex and edge fields are identified only
through the displayed map #{j}.}

\p{For #{f,g\in\mathcal F_R(V)},
##{\Delta(fg)=(\Delta f)(Tg)+f\Delta g.}
Equivalently,
##{\Delta(fg)=(\Delta f)g+f\Delta g+(\Delta f)(\Delta g).}
Wingate defines forward and backward differences with lattice spacing
#{a} in \citet{Section 4, Equations (4.2)-(4.3), p. 3}{wingate2025lattice}.
Here #{a=1}; the ring-valued finite identities are direct calculations,
with no continuum approximation.}

\proof{
\p{For every #{i}, #{(jh^*f)(i)=f(i+1)=Tf(i)} and
#{(js^*f)(i)=f(i)}. Applying the product-preserving map #{j} to
\ref{fdip-0012} therefore gives the first formula.
Substitute #{Tg=g+\Delta g} to obtain the second. The inverse-shift
formula follows by adding and subtracting one modulo #{n}.}
}

\p{At #{n=1}, the cycle has a loop and #{T=I}, #{\Delta=0}.
At #{n=2}, its two edges have opposite orientations between the same
vertices; it is not a single simple edge. The [four-cycle](fdip-0003)
exhibits failure of the unshifted Leibniz rule. The separate
[counting-adjoint calculation](fdip-0014) adds a real pairing
and identifies the positive kinetic factorization.}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{fwdDiff_smul} proves the three-term formula for
an additive commutative monoid, a ring and a module over it. Take the
index monoid #{\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z}, step #{1}, and the regular
#{R}-module #{R}. Scalar multiplication is ring multiplication.
Substituting #{Tg=g+\Delta g} yields the shifted formula; this marker
does not claim the endpoint incidence or counting-adjoint theorem.}
